Arrogant ignorance is when a character knows less than everyone else and acts like that makes them the authority. The way to play it is to give them something they genuinely want, then make every correction deepen their commitment. A guy at his first yoga class hears “downward dog” and says, calmly, “I’ve worked with dogs. Let me handle this.”

That’s a comedy game. Once the audience catches the pattern, they can anticipate the next move. The fun is seeing how it happens this time.

Comedy games and how to play them

Game	How to play it	Example
Arrogant ignorance	Make a wrong assumption with total confidence. Treat correction as proof that everyone else needs your help.	“Nobody here has established dominance over the downward dog.”
Overcompetence	Give a genuinely capable person a tiny task. Let them apply all their expertise at full strength.	Dad briefs the route, contingency plans, and snack reserves for a walk to the mailbox.
Status reversal	Establish who’s in charge. Give the least powerful person control over the one thing everyone needs.	The whole household negotiates with a four-year-old who knows where the TV remote is.
Literalism	Take a figure of speech at face value and follow through with practical questions.	“We need boots on the ground.” “How many boots? Are people still in them?”
Wrong frame	Apply the rules of one world to another. Make the comparison precise enough to sustain several beats.	A sports commentator covers two people trying to carry a couch through a doorway. The replay reveals who stopped listening.
Petty stakes, epic commitment	Make a trivial objective sacred to the character. Let the effort become wildly disproportionate.	Two brothers haven’t spoken in three years because one said “easy” after losing at cornhole.
The cover-up	Give someone a small, forgivable mistake. Every attempt to hide it creates a bigger problem.	He forgets a coworker’s name and starts calling everyone “Chief” to establish that it’s his thing.
The reasonable extremist	Start with something sensible. Follow the same logic past every normal stopping point.	“I’m cutting down on how many drinks I carry.” Next scene, he’s pitching a hydration utility belt.
The straight man finally breaks	Let one person calmly absorb the nonsense. When they finally join in, make them the most committed person in the room.	The nurse rolls her eyes at the Beverage Goblin all shift. At closing, she pulls out a laminated ranking of every drink he offers.
The audience knows	Let viewers see the mistake early. Give the character an innocent reason to keep walking toward it.	Everybody sees that Dad’s sample “private” voice memo is playing over the car speakers. Dad gets to the part where he does impressions.
Repetition with variation	Repeat a recognizable beat. Change its meaning or consequence each time.	“I know a shortcut” gets you a faster route, then a dirt road, then a ferry the driver insists was always part of the plan.
Underreaction	Put something outrageous in frame. Give the character a specific, ordinary concern.	A dragon lands on the roof. The homeowner looks up and says, “Those gutters went in Tuesday.”

Style is how you deliver the game

Take one premise. A guy mistakes yoga for dog training.

* Deadpan: He quietly asks the instructor how long she’s been having trouble with the dog.
* Farce: He’s already called a trainer. The trainer is at the door. He has thirty seconds to convince the class there’s a dog.
* Mockumentary: In a confessional, he says, “I recognized pretty early that this was a terminology issue.” Cut to him saying “sit” to the instructor.
* Satire: He renames the exact same yoga class Tactical Mobility, charges triple, and sells out.
* Absurdism: An actual dog walks in, corrects his form, and leaves.
* Physical comedy: He tries to demonstrate his expertise, gets stuck in the pose, and starts issuing orders from the floor.

How to make up your own

Start with an ordinary situation. Give one person a rule they will keep following. Play the first beat so we understand the rule, the second so we recognize it, and the third so we see a consequence we didn’t predict.

Here’s one built from your yoga observation. The rule is he’ll do any exercise if the name sounds tactical enough.

“I don’t do child’s pose.”
“That’s a low-profile recovery position.”
He gets straight down on the mat.

“I’m definitely not doing happy baby.”
“Supine hip deployment.”
He grabs both feet. “How many reps?”

His wife walks in. “Will you unload the dishwasher?”
“In a minute.”
“Kitchen resupply.”
He stands up. “Plates first?”

That third beat is where you find out how much life the game has. His wife learns the rule and uses it. The joke can now travel to a new room, a new character can play it, and the audience can start inventing their own versions.